{"subjectId":"data-science","id":"regression","title":"Polynomial Regression","group":"Machine Learning","order":11,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Linear regression fits a straight line. When the relationship curves, add powers of the input (x², x³…) as extra features — the model stays linear in its coefficients, but the fitted curve bends.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Polynomial features</strong> — turning <code>[x]</code> into <code>[1, x, x²]</code> (degree 2). <code>PolynomialFeatures</code> does this; <code>LinearRegression</code> then fits <code>y ≈ b₀ + b₁x + b₂x²</code>.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n\nX = np.array([[1], [2], [3], [4], [5]])\ny = np.array([3, 8, 9, 70, 80])\n\npoly = PolynomialFeatures(degree=2)\nX_poly = poly.fit_transform(X)\nprint(X_poly[1])                                  # [1. 2. 4.]   ← 1, x, x² for x = 2\n\nmodel = LinearRegression().fit(X_poly, y)\nprint(model.predict(poly.transform([[6]])).round(1))   # [133.8]\nprint(round(model.score(X_poly, y), 3))           # 0.884</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-git-merge\"></i>Cleaner with a pipeline</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n\nX = np.array([[1], [2], [3], [4], [5]])\ny = np.array([3, 8, 9, 70, 80])\nmodel = make_pipeline(PolynomialFeatures(degree=2), LinearRegression())\nmodel.fit(X, y)                                   # transform + fit in one step\nprint(model.predict([[6]]).round(1))              # [133.8]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-line\"></i>Plotting the fit</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n\nX = np.array([[1], [2], [3], [4], [5]])\ny = np.array([3, 8, 9, 70, 80])\nmodel = make_pipeline(PolynomialFeatures(degree=2), LinearRegression()).fit(X, y)\n\nX_range = np.linspace(0, 6, 100).reshape(-1, 1)   # 100 points to draw a smooth curve\nplt.scatter(X, y, label=\"Data points\")\nplt.plot(X_range, model.predict(X_range), color=\"red\", label=\"Polynomial fit\")\nplt.xlabel(\"X\"); plt.ylabel(\"y\"); plt.legend()\nplt.show()</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Higher degree isn't better: with 5 points, degree 4 passes through every point exactly and predicts nonsense between and beyond them. Choose the degree by testing on held-out data.</p></div></section>"}